{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011Cgfzg3Y1FAnmuEpTEMJYF","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"For d³ in an octahedral field (⁴A₂g ground state), the three spin-allowed bands relative to the ground state are:\n\n- ν₁ = ⁴A₂ → ⁴T₂ = 10Dq\n- ν₂, ν₃ = ⁴A₂ → ⁴T₁(F), ⁴T₁(P) = 7.5B + 15Dq ∓ ½√(225B² − 180B·Dq + 100Dq²)\n\nThese quartet energies do not depend on C. So the assumption C/B = 4.5 only sets C; it does not change the fit.\n\n**Quick hand estimate**\n- From ν₁: Dq = 12 300 / 10 = 1230 cm⁻¹.\n- From the sum rule ν₂ + ν₃ = 15B + 30Dq: 46 400 = 15B + 36 900, so B ≈ 633 cm⁻¹.\n\nThese values put ν₃ − ν₂ at about 10 060 cm⁻¹, but the observed gap is 9400 cm⁻¹. The three bands therefore cannot all be matched exactly, and a least-squares fit is the better approach.\n\n**Least-squares fit to all three bands (full d³ matrices)**\n- Dq ≈ 1228 cm⁻¹ (10Dq ≈ 12 280 cm⁻¹)\n- B ≈ 628 cm⁻¹\n- Predicted bands: 12 284, 18 116 and 28 154 cm⁻¹. Residuals are −16, −384 and +254 cm⁻¹ (RMS about 270 cm⁻¹, R² = 0.998).\n- C = 4.5B ≈ 2825 cm⁻¹. This is an assumed value; the bands do not determine it.\n\nB ≈ 628 cm⁻¹ is about 82% of the free-ion V²⁺ value (about 766 cm⁻¹), so β ≈ 0.82. That modest nephelauxetic reduction is typical of an aqua complex.\n\n{\"Dq\": 1228, \"B\": 628, \"C\": 2825, \"out_of_scope\": false, \"reason\": \"Least-squares fit of the three spin-allowed d3 bands; C taken as 4.5B and not determined by these bands.\"}"}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1919,"cache_read_input_tokens":8038,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":1919,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":3,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"e9ed42a0-d2d2-427e-8e3e-c488bb7656fe",